JAHS-Bench-201 query bridge memory (2026-09-15, measurement/resources.py, whole process tree)
Machine: i7-11800H, 31.7 GB RAM, Windows 11.

cifar10, random_search, 20 queries in one bridge:
  peak resident memory (loading the surrogate models): 17.47 GB
  resident memory after loading:                         1.85 GB
  first query (includes loading):                      ~224 s

Consequences for the parallel pipeline:
  * one bridge loads at a time: lock locks/jahs-bridge-load.lock
    (substrates/jahs_bench_201.py, P3NET_JAHS_LOAD_LOCK);
  * per JAHS worker after loading: ~1.9 GB bridge + 0.6-2.7 GB worker process;
  * 3 workers, worst case: 17.5 + 2 x 1.9 + 3 x 2.7 = ~29 GB of 31.7 GB
    -> plan default workers: 3.

CORRECTION (2026-09-20, Linux, Hetzner CPX62, 30 GB RAM)
--------------------------------------------------------
The 1.85 GB "resident after loading" above is a Windows working-set artifact.
On Linux the same bridge holds ~11.7 GB of anonymous memory per loaded
dataset, measured as anon-rss of the processes the OOM killer reported:

  Out of memory: Killed process 32437 (python) total-vm:14969716kB,
  anon-rss:11690964kB   (and two more, 11.71 GB and 11.59 GB)

With one bridge per worker this exhausted 30 GB at three workers: six OOM
kills in an hour at 6 and 8 workers, before any Vizier run had started.

Consequence: all workers on a machine now share ONE bridge over a local
socket (P3NET_JAHS_SERVER_DIR, substrates/jahs_bench_201.py), which keeps at
most `parallel.jahs_max_datasets` datasets loaded (default 1). Memory is then
per dataset, not per worker:

  12 GB (one dataset) + 13 GB (one OSS Vizier run) + ~0.5 GB per worker

Verified: p3net on jahs_bench_201, budget 50, seed 1 through the shared
bridge reproduces the archived run element for element.
